<h2>The joint venture and the dispute</h2>
<p>The underlying relationship was a 2008 share purchase and shareholders agreement under which a public sector trading company held 26% and its foreign partner the majority stake in an Indian joint venture operating a gold and silver refinery. The petitioner&rsquo;s case before the tribunal was that the majority shareholder used its control to route payments of roughly Rs 71 crore from the joint venture to its own affiliates under a sourcing agreement and a know-how licence, for services the petitioner said the partner was obliged to provide without separate charge, in breach of the agreement&rsquo;s good-faith clause. The tribunal dismissed all claims in December 2016, finding the agreement did not require free services and that the affiliate transactions were at arm&rsquo;s length, and awarded substantial costs. The Section 34 petition spent the better part of a decade on the court&rsquo;s board before judgment.</p>

<h2>The narrowed battlefield</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Grounds pressed.</strong> Misinterpretation of the contract, a perverse arm&rsquo;s-length finding, and patent illegality in the treatment of the good-faith obligation.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Grounds available.</strong> With a Mauritius-incorporated respondent, only the Section 34(2) grounds applied — the court held, in terms, that patent illegality &ldquo;is not an available ground for challenging an award arising out of an international commercial arbitration.&rdquo;</div>
</div>

<h2>The decisive gap in the award</h2>
<p>What remained was the public-policy and natural-justice family of grounds, and here the challenge found its mark. The court reasoned that compliance with the agreement&rsquo;s good-faith obligation was a mandatory condition precedent to any adjudication of whether the affiliate transactions were at arm&rsquo;s length. Yet the award, examined as a whole, showed the good-faith defence was left completely unadjudicated. Drawing on recent Division Bench authority of the same court, the judgment treats a complete failure to decide a material issue — as distinct from deciding it badly or briefly — as falling within Sections 34(2)(a)(iii)/(iv) and 34(2)(b)(ii), where it results in actual prejudice. The omission here, the court found, directly vitiated the tribunal&rsquo;s final decision.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;an arbitral award cannot be set aside simply because the arbitrator has committed an error of law&rdquo; — the interference rested not on error, but on the issue never having been decided at all.</p>
</div>

<h2>The relief — carefully tailored</h2>
<p>The operative order is an exercise in surgical restraint. The award was set aside to the extent that it dismissed the surviving claims and, consequentially, directed the petitioner to pay costs premised on that dismissal. Findings on prayers the petitioner had abandoned before the tribunal were left intact, and the parties were expressly left free to pursue contractual or other legal remedies for the surviving dispute. The judgment thus avoids both extremes: it neither rewrites the award nor lets a half-decided one stand.</p>

<h2>Lessons for arbitration users</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For tribunals.</strong> Issues framed must be issues answered. Silence on a condition-precedent question is the kind of defect that survives even the narrow international-arbitration standard of review.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For award challengers.</strong> Identify the ground that actually fits: in international commercial arbitrations, perversity arguments must be translated into the Section 34(2) vocabulary of natural justice and public policy, or they fail at the threshold.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For award holders.</strong> A decade-long Section 34 pendency is a reminder to seek early listing and press for enforcement-stage safeguards; the finality of an award is only as good as the speed of the challenge process.</div>
</div>
